1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,250
Farming Simulator is a game where you wake
up...

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:04,450
go to work...

3
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:05,770
make money...

4
00:00:05,820 --> 00:00:07,850
buy extremely expensive equipment...

5
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:08,560
and then...

6
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:10,670
go back to work.

7
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:15,132
Which sounds suspiciously similar to the
thing video games were invented to help us

8
00:00:15,132 --> 00:00:15,770
escape from.

9
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:17,250
There are no dragons.

10
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:18,610
No gunfights.

11
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:20,450
No princess to save.

12
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,620
Most of the time, your greatest enemy...

13
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:25,700
is a field that's slightly larger than you
expected.

14
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:29,120
And yet millions of people voluntarily
spend their evenings

15
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:30,470
planting crops,

16
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:32,150
transporting fertilizer,

17
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:33,530
feeding animals...

18
00:00:33,580 --> 00:00:36,930
and getting genuinely excited about buying
a bigger tractor.

19
00:00:36,980 --> 00:00:37,360
So...

20
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:39,180
why?

21
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:44,400
Most video games sell us fantasies.

22
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:48,928
Grand Theft Auto lets you become a
criminal with an apparently unlimited

23
00:00:48,928 --> 00:00:50,740
ability to survive car crashes.

24
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,580
Call of Duty turns you into a soldier.

25
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:58,440
Minecraft gives you an entire world and
basically says: “Good luck.”

26
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:00,260
Farming Simulator gives you...

27
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:01,920
a loan.

28
00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:02,790
A tractor.

29
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:05,340
And a field that needs cultivating.

30
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:06,350
That's it.

31
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:07,410
Congratulations.

32
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:10,340
Your fantasy is agricultural debt.

33
00:01:10,390 --> 00:01:12,670
But here's where things get interesting.

34
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:18,270
Because Farming Simulator isn't really
selling the fantasy of being a farmer.

35
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:21,090
It's selling something much more powerful.

36
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:26,080
The fantasy that your work actually makes
sense.

37
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:30,940
Think about how progress works in most
people's real lives.

38
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:32,070
You work all week.

39
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:33,170
You answer emails.

40
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:34,330
You attend meetings.

41
00:01:34,380 --> 00:01:36,000
You solve problems.

42
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:36,860
Friday arrives...

43
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:40,920
and somehow there are now more emails.

44
00:01:41,460 --> 00:01:43,630
Progress can be difficult to see.

45
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:46,090
Farming Simulator has no such problem.

46
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:47,510
You cultivate a field.

47
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:49,810
You can see the field change.

48
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:51,010
You plant something.

49
00:01:51,060 --> 00:01:52,110
It grows.

50
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:53,330
You harvest it.

51
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:54,180
You sell it.

52
00:01:54,180 --> 00:01:57,310
The number in your bank account goes up.

53
00:01:57,360 --> 00:01:58,840
Then you buy a better machine.

54
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:02,040
And now you can do the exact same job...

55
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:03,540
faster.

56
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:05,610
Which shouldn't be exciting.

57
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:06,750
But it is.

58
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:09,810
Because every action has a visible result.

59
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:12,160
There is almost no ambiguity.

60
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:13,330
Field empty.

61
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:14,230
You work.

62
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:15,480
Field full.

63
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:16,780
Brain happy.

64
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:20,220
And that simple loop is incredibly
satisfying.

65
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:24,690
This is where Farming Simulator starts to
reveal what it really is.

66
00:02:24,740 --> 00:02:27,230
The crops aren't necessarily the important
part.

67
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:29,230
The important part is the loop.

68
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:30,110
Start small.

69
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:30,830
Work.

70
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:31,810
Earn.

71
00:02:31,860 --> 00:02:32,390
Improve.

72
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:33,370
Expand.

73
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:33,970
Repeat.

74
00:02:34,020 --> 00:02:37,990
It's the same basic structure behind an
enormous number of successful games.

75
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:41,370
Except Farming Simulator removes almost
everything else.

76
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:44,550
No giant boss suddenly appears and
destroys your wheat.

77
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:46,850
Nobody invades your farm while you're
sleeping.

78
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:50,420
Your tractor does not develop a tragic
backstory halfway through the game.

79
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:51,160
You just...

80
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:52,670
make progress.

81
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:54,020
Slowly.

82
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:54,690
Predictably.

83
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:57,590
And for a lot of people, that's incredibly
relaxing.

84
00:02:57,640 --> 00:02:59,190
But there's another reason this works.

85
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:01,620
And it's probably the strangest one.

86
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:06,680
Video games have somehow convinced us that
chores are entertainment.

87
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:10,030
Minecraft asks you to spend hours digging
holes.

88
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:13,420
House Flipper lets you clean other
people's houses.

89
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:18,010
PowerWash Simulator made an entire game
out of removing dirt.

90
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:23,894
Euro Truck Simulator asks you to drive a
truck across Europe while obeying traffic

91
00:03:23,894 --> 00:03:24,250
laws.

92
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:29,434
And Farming Simulator asks you to spend
your free time managing agricultural

93
00:03:29,434 --> 00:03:30,110
equipment.

94
00:03:30,160 --> 00:03:33,740
If someone gave you these tasks in real
life...

95
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:36,210
you’d probably ask how much they were
paying.

96
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:38,060
But put a progress bar on them...

97
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:41,810
and suddenly we're having a wonderful
evening.

98
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:42,650
Why?

99
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:47,550
Because a chore stops feeling like a chore
when three things change.

100
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:49,170
You chose to do it.

101
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:51,330
You can stop whenever you want.

102
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:53,770
And the reward is clear.

103
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:57,960
Nobody is forcing you to harvest that
field at 11 p.m.

104
00:03:58,010 --> 00:04:00,780
You are harvesting that field at 11 p.m...

105
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:03,180
because you have plans.

106
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:04,620
Big plans.

107
00:04:04,740 --> 00:04:07,840
That second harvester isn't going to buy
itself.

108
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:10,610
Real life is messy.

109
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:13,640
You can do everything correctly and still
fail.

110
00:04:13,940 --> 00:04:14,550
Traffic happens.

111
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:16,150
People change their minds.

112
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:17,250
Prices move.

113
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:18,650
Plans collapse.

114
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:23,933
Your internet provider decides today is
apparently the perfect day to discover new

115
00:04:23,933 --> 00:04:26,230
and creative ways to disappoint you.

116
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:29,210
But simulation games give you a smaller
world.

117
00:04:29,260 --> 00:04:31,300
A world you can understand.

118
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:32,590
Plant at the right time.

119
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:33,890
Use the right equipment.

120
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:34,990
Manage your money.

121
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:35,780
Do the work.

122
00:04:35,780 --> 00:04:37,130
And generally...

123
00:04:37,180 --> 00:04:39,170
things happen for a reason.

124
00:04:39,220 --> 00:04:42,370
That gives the player something
surprisingly valuable:

125
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:43,370
control.

126
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:45,190
Not absolute control.

127
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:49,987
Farming Simulator still has weather,
economics, machinery and plenty of ways to

128
00:04:49,987 --> 00:04:52,030
make terrible financial decisions.

129
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:54,190
But it's understandable chaos.

130
00:04:54,240 --> 00:04:56,510
The kind of chaos you can organize.

131
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:59,250
And humans really like organizing chaos.

132
00:04:59,300 --> 00:05:01,620
It's basically half of civilization.

133
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:07,020
Of course, we also have to address the
enormous green elephant in the room.

134
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:09,080
Tractors are cool.

135
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:12,530
Farming Simulator understands this
extremely well.

136
00:05:12,580 --> 00:05:15,850
The game is filled with real agricultural
machinery.

137
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:17,150
Tiny tractors.

138
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:18,470
Huge tractors.

139
00:05:18,520 --> 00:05:22,270
Harvesters that look like industrial
buildings learned how to move.

140
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:23,910
Machines for planting.

141
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:25,530
Machines for spraying.

142
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:26,620
Machines for cutting.

143
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:31,258
Machines whose purpose you will
confidently pretend to understand five

144
00:05:31,258 --> 00:05:33,510
minutes after watching a tutorial.

145
00:05:33,560 --> 00:05:36,770
And this creates another powerful
progression system.

146
00:05:36,820 --> 00:05:38,770
You don't just earn more money.

147
00:05:38,820 --> 00:05:40,950
You earn better tools.

148
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,610
At first, a large field can feel like a
project.

149
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:49,893
Later, you arrive with a machine worth
more than a house and erase the problem in

150
00:05:49,893 --> 00:05:50,410
minutes.

151
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:52,610
That's progression you can feel.

152
00:05:52,660 --> 00:05:54,490
The game doesn't have to tell you:

153
00:05:54,540 --> 00:05:55,900
“You are stronger now.”

154
00:05:55,900 --> 00:05:58,870
Your tractor is twelve meters wider.

155
00:05:58,920 --> 00:06:00,880
You already know.

156
00:06:01,420 --> 00:06:02,710
And eventually...

157
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:04,490
something else happens.

158
00:06:04,540 --> 00:06:06,280
The farm becomes yours.

159
00:06:06,460 --> 00:06:07,950
You chose the fields.

160
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:09,430
You bought the machines.

161
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:11,190
You decided what to grow.

162
00:06:11,240 --> 00:06:12,730
You placed the buildings.

163
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:14,170
You added animals.

164
00:06:14,220 --> 00:06:16,250
You connected production chains.

165
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:20,810
Maybe you started with one tractor and a
questionable financial situation.

166
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:22,930
Now you've built an operation.

167
00:06:22,980 --> 00:06:25,290
And once you've spent hours building
something...

168
00:06:25,340 --> 00:06:28,490
it stops feeling like a collection of game
mechanics.

169
00:06:28,540 --> 00:06:30,560
It becomes a place.

170
00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:31,910
Your place.

171
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:35,770
Which is also why starting again can feel
weirdly exciting.

172
00:06:35,820 --> 00:06:37,970
An empty farm isn't empty.

173
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:39,340
It's potential.

174
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,860
There's another thing Farming Simulator
does that sounds almost illegal in modern

175
00:06:44,860 --> 00:06:45,700
entertainment.

176
00:06:45,820 --> 00:06:47,460
It's slow.

177
00:06:47,540 --> 00:06:48,550
Really slow.

178
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:50,830
There are moments where nothing dramatic
happens.

179
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:52,080
You just drive.

180
00:06:52,080 --> 00:06:54,090
Back and forth.

181
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:55,440
Back and forth.

182
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:55,800
Back...

183
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:57,570
and forth.

184
00:06:57,620 --> 00:06:59,730
And that's not necessarily a flaw.

185
00:06:59,780 --> 00:07:03,240
Most entertainment is fighting desperately
for our attention.

186
00:07:03,540 --> 00:07:03,990
Messages.

187
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:05,180
Notifications.

188
00:07:05,340 --> 00:07:06,010
Shorts.

189
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:07,090
News.

190
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:07,730
Videos.

191
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:08,480
Another video.

192
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:13,500
Someone explaining why the thing you liked
yesterday is actually terrible.

193
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:15,770
Farming Simulator doesn't really care.

194
00:07:15,820 --> 00:07:17,250
The wheat will be there.

195
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:18,650
You can take your time.

196
00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:23,417
There's something almost meditative about
completing a simple task when nothing else

197
00:07:23,417 --> 00:07:25,270
is asking you to make a decision.

198
00:07:25,320 --> 00:07:26,570
Your hands are busy.

199
00:07:26,620 --> 00:07:27,890
Your brain isn't.

200
00:07:27,940 --> 00:07:28,930
And sometimes...

201
00:07:28,980 --> 00:07:31,840
that's exactly what people want from a
game.

202
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:34,730
Which raises an obvious question.

203
00:07:34,780 --> 00:07:37,250
If farming is so relaxing...

204
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:39,710
why not just farm in real life?

205
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:42,250
Because real farming is hard.

206
00:07:42,300 --> 00:07:43,330
Very hard.

207
00:07:43,380 --> 00:07:44,310
It's physical.

208
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:45,350
Expensive.

209
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:46,450
Risky.

210
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:47,430
Weather matters.

211
00:07:47,480 --> 00:07:48,590
Markets matter.

212
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:50,070
Equipment breaks.

213
00:07:50,120 --> 00:07:54,660
Animals do not have convenient status bars
floating above their heads.

214
00:07:54,780 --> 00:07:57,550
Farming Simulator takes a complicated
profession...

215
00:07:57,600 --> 00:07:59,760
keeps many of the satisfying systems...

216
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:03,020
and removes most of the consequences.

217
00:08:03,220 --> 00:08:05,900
It's farming with a save button.

218
00:08:06,340 --> 00:08:10,838
Agriculture where your worst financial
decision can occasionally be solved by

219
00:08:10,838 --> 00:08:11,890
loading yesterday.

220
00:08:11,940 --> 00:08:14,460
The simulator keeps the satisfying
systems...

221
00:08:14,460 --> 00:08:17,280
and gives you a second chance.

222
00:08:18,040 --> 00:08:21,260
And that brings us back to the original
question.

223
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:25,670
Why do millions of people play a game
about farming?

224
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:29,710
Maybe because Farming Simulator isn't
really about farming.

225
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:32,310
It's about progress you can see.

226
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:34,090
Problems you can solve.

227
00:08:34,140 --> 00:08:36,230
A world you can understand.

228
00:08:36,280 --> 00:08:38,410
Work you chose to do.

229
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:41,570
And effort that reliably becomes
something.

230
00:08:41,620 --> 00:08:42,670
You plant.

231
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:43,830
Something grows.

232
00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:44,870
You harvest.

233
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:46,350
Something improves.

234
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:49,510
There's something deeply satisfying about
that.

235
00:08:49,560 --> 00:08:53,780
Especially because real life doesn't
always work that way.

236
00:08:53,940 --> 00:08:54,990
So yes.

237
00:08:55,200 --> 00:08:59,650
Farming Simulator is a game where millions
of people finish a day of work...

238
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:00,850
sit down...

239
00:09:00,900 --> 00:09:02,830
and start working again.

240
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:04,380
Which still sounds ridiculous.

241
00:09:04,380 --> 00:09:07,980
Until you notice one important difference.

242
00:09:08,180 --> 00:09:11,310
Real work often tells you what needs to be
done.

243
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:13,120
Farming Simulator asks...

244
00:09:13,260 --> 00:09:14,880
what do you want to build?

245
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:19,650
And maybe that's why doing chores can
somehow feel like an escape.

246
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:22,930
Because the fantasy was never becoming a
farmer.

247
00:09:22,980 --> 00:09:23,740
The fantasy...

248
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:28,730
was living in a world where effort always
leaves a mark.

249
00:09:28,780 --> 00:09:29,750
And apparently...

250
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:31,900
sometimes that world just needs...

251
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:34,460
a really expensive tractor.

252
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:36,570
And now I want to know:

253
00:09:36,620 --> 00:09:39,670
What game deserves a “why?” next?

254
00:09:39,720 --> 00:09:41,250
Tell me in the comments.

255
00:09:41,300 --> 00:09:43,640
Maybe that’s our next quest.
